# Line endings — LF everywhere, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# ── Why this file exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CI run #10 failed on a commit whose tests passed locally, and the difference
# was line endings. This repository was checked out CRLF on Windows (the default
# `core.autocrlf=true`) and LF on the Linux runner, so the same commit was
# different bytes on the two machines.
#
# That matters here because several tools pattern-match across line breaks.
# `scripts/claims.mjs` split paragraphs on `\n{2,}`, and a CRLF blank line is
# `\r\n\r\n` — the two newlines are not adjacent, so the split never fired on
# Windows. The identical content measured 31 unsourced claims on Windows and 34
# on Linux. The claims ratchet is the number this project has tracked across
# five runs; every baseline ever recorded was a Windows-only measurement, and CI
# had been comparing against a figure it did not produce.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than plain `text=auto`: `text=auto` normalises the repository
# but still hands Windows a CRLF working tree, which leaves the two machines
# reading different bytes and the bug intact. Forcing LF in the checkout is what
# makes "same commit, same answer" true.
#
# The scripts also normalise defensively (readContent, and sentences() in
# claims.mjs), and tests/sourcing.test.mts asserts the classifier is
# line-ending invariant. This file removes the cause; those remove the class.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries: never touched, never normalised. Several of these are committed
# build outputs — the font subsets and the WebP images — and a byte-for-byte
# guarantee is what makes `npm run determinism` meaningful.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
